 between its endpoints.\n\nAmong the given options, the only one that satisfies the definition is the following:\n\n![](images/68/682efe4cf72bd72fc0acb7f4b6ff201ae4db59bb4dfc28d6359a04df7abfe48b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395520 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d15f8fa8-f265-50da-b172-3ccb90895448" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Connected Regions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f357254-f769-5436-bd0b-25358181a0f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d0/d0abe9cb42672156fe48a934e1c9337af315d16d8afd75c8092ca9a01657e922.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395522} {:answer/id #uuid "bed2a6a9-752e-5e4f-8000-ecbcb240a187" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/12/12c8be4491fe81d8ccf4ea964d883f2dc079f5183b72a8cd5b38260b8d3229d5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395523} {:answer/id #uuid "f3c05222-2416-5b40-9ab2-a005052e8eca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a1/a1e81bf264c71d1e4b14ea7a4f845c8276626e49238e10275afd9a1eeee22eec.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395524} {:answer/id #uuid "0447b677-359b-5b6f-a358-d3e5ef69c66d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b5/b54f31e2780f3f6c11ecc69eaab5dee59ddf2ba0c542f1e9bbee4f8fe25284ea.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395525} {:answer/id #uuid "bfabcff4-75db-540e-9198-01c700bc07c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a6/a6b83ae381ebc092e930f588b7ccd0851d09b521664285d700157c7091794fc2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395526}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bed2a6a9-752e-5e4f-8000-ecbcb240a187" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/12/12c8be4491fe81d8ccf4ea964d883f2dc079f5183b72a8cd5b38260b8d3229d5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395523} :answer-field/id #uuid "d30dfba9-dad9-5198-8051-754b14711f5e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395521}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "82dba76d-c396-5269-a0c3-fa0f434c28a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-98398" :question/problem "Which of the following regions is *not* connected?" :question/worked-solution "A region $D$ is connected if every pair of points in $D$ can be joined by a smooth curve or a path in $D.$ \n\nAmong the given options, the only one that does not satisfy this definition is the following:\n\n  \n\n![](images/12/12c8be4491fe81d8ccf4ea964d883f2dc079f5183b72a8cd5b38260b8d3229d5.png)\n\nNote that if we take two points $P$ and $Q$, as shown below, there is no path lying entirely within the region that connects them.\n\n   \n\n![](images/b3/b3dd1c29d770f36e4542427175eab7e102ee3e43a944d525459d28257e6d24a7.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395527 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66924d52-fd39-5308-b78e-611e3d3129ad" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Line That Corresponds to a Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "298e0721-a46f-5ceb-9c85-f931ed02acf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/63/638f98960fbcfac5d0f10385037b92479fdab4177811f578d89bd997d7db2353.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395529} {:answer/id #uuid "1570cbd9-d5a9-51e5-9f83-3089fceb3038" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7f/7f44aa29a8d99f144707d3126102ad633fd8b0f947ae16d862870444a5125793.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395530} {:answer/id #uuid "4b6a9b00-ac8c-5699-8ea3-9b92478b55f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b0/b08c48590fe2538666e0e3a559c123b0f5cd3bb35680f2c98d3c14691780a575.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395531} {:answer/id #uuid "e13e11d4-d69d-53ec-9487-a8851fd5e492" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/99/99e226354d8d1c48e11545d818083eb378ab931fd536e9a0389141e474ab27ee.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395532} {:answer/id #uuid "81174c62-81ee-55df-8611-d6e354b46ba6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8d/8d7a4b4ac510e040a6d6ca87cf96e72c30a298ea450b0154d6080b9e73a19e8b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395533}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b6a9b00-ac8c-5699-8ea3-9b92478b55f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b0/b08c48590fe2538666e0e3a559c123b0f5cd3bb35680f2c98d3c14691780a575.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395531} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5cb958b-c045-57d0-bdf5-09c3de6ac9b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395528}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "796023e8-480d-5ead-996f-d9e2e236082e" :question/math-academy-id "q-98527" :question/problem "Which of the following is the phase line of the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-{y}^{2}-12y-35?$" :question/worked-solution "To draw the phase line of the differential equation, we proceed using the following three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation. \n\nIn this case, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{y}^{2}-12y-35 & =0 \\\\ -({y}^{2}+12y+35) & =0 \\\\ -(y+7)(y+5) & =0 \\\\ y & =-7,-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium solutions are $y=-7$ and $y=-5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the intervals of $y$ for which $f(y)>0$ and $f(y)<0.$ \n\nConstructing a sign table, we get the following:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-7)$ | $(-7,-5)$ | $(-5,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-(y+7)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $y+5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-7,-5),$ and negative for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-7)\\cup (-5,\\infty ).$\n\n**Step 3**: Draw the phase line using the following recipe:\n\n- Draw a vertical $y$-axis\n\n- Mark the equilibrium solutions\n\n- Draw arrows pointing up in the intervals where $f(y)>0$\n\n- Draw arrows pointing down in the intervals where $f(y)<0$\n\nApplying the above recipe, we get the following phase line for our differential equation.\n\n![](images/b0/b08c48590fe2538666e0e3a559c123b0f5cd3bb35680f2c98d3c14691780a575.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395534 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66924d52-fd39-5308-b78e-611e3d3129ad" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Line That Corresponds to a Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "090719f1-8499-5e9d-b61c-1dab1a7eaab0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/66/66b90a2d1509ddb46e27d3e7164dbe1ab9b1f42aefd5df4b4482d8d2046f9bba.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395536} {:answer/id #uuid "1d02280f-ba3e-552e-a757-4b2ff6d3e67c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/5f/5fa18a0300f34e5a471dac55a30a1edc838717ef154f3f776f6fabe08536a3e6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395537} {:answer/id #uuid "37cf5aee-2a01-568a-ba64-446a635d03cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/fc/fca94bef82d6e693a93796fe353fc0efacbba86f6b51c59d1fe7df7932ffd912.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395538} {:answer/id #uuid "81e2da43-747e-5608-8968-53a516724969" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7b/7b948fa43dc2f8a60f8e8df9d02ae65245891ae9ddb34ab8928b9dc696e36cc4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395539} {:answer/id #uuid "135b8596-5a73-574c-9298-ff3f4ad1bfc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a8/a8ee4e28944f944050c9378f62c72924219efb44e6b26c51f8a8ebc6b9300528.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395540}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d02280f-ba3e-552e-a757-4b2ff6d3e67c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/5f/5fa18a0300f34e5a471dac55a30a1edc838717ef154f3f776f6fabe08536a3e6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395537} :answer-field/id #uuid "27e889b3-4a89-5bdb-bd74-2ca245c80e34" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395535}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e4c26980-2b20-50dd-83dd-2c79a12fb753" :question/math-academy-id "q-98556" :question/problem "Which of the following is the phase line of the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{3}-4y?$" :question/worked-solution "To draw the phase line of the differential equation, we proceed using the following three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation. \n\nFirst, we factor the function $f(y),$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{3}-4y & =0 \\\\ y(y-2)(y+2) & =0 \\\\ y & =-2,0,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium solutions are $y=-2,$ $y=0$ and $y=2.$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the intervals of $y$ for which $f(y)>0$ and $f(y)<0.$ \n\nIn this case, we have \n    \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{3}-4y & >0 \\\\ y(y-2)(y+2) & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nConstructing a sign table, we get the following:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y+2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-2,0)\\cup (2,\\infty ),$ and negative for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (0,2).$\n\n**Step 3**: Draw the phase line using the following recipe:\n\n- Draw a vertical $y$-axis\n\n- Mark the equilibrium solutions\n\n- Draw arrows pointing up in the intervals where $f(y)>0$\n\n- Draw arrows pointing down in the intervals where $f(y)<0$\n\nApplying the above recipe, we get the following phase line for our differential equation.\n\n![](images/5f/5fa18a0300f34e5a471dac55a30a1edc838717ef154f3f776f6fabe08536a3e6.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395541 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "680a42fd-9b70-5fb2-916c-d10b71b11d82" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Equilibrium Points Using Phase Lines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e5012c2-5230-5873-92a9-6488c8bfae5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/86/86978b5735f85962ae769e1781029b25b30b943ab274d29b93aee80521839c1f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395543} {:answer/id #uuid "8234414a-b377-5a1f-8fa0-ae1810f24c4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/dd/dd7fd07ac56b96a71ff95444c99b21f3d862f8ab485495b501fea366fa15a8ab.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395544} {:answer/id #uuid "b6f5c1bb-8e32-515e-9bbd-0eb29aa53a5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/82/82bb7d8cf05f050c733cb65632807ab14f290f57bdb033958759c54ccbb5f930.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395545} {:answer/id #uuid "eaf619c5-ca27-565a-85dd-948767c05061" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8a/8a5f3a5965878cba364ee0e96af77bea41d53e6b2bd440340d72215e30db3a83.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395546} {:answer/id #uuid "c9cc7a04-4036-5f92-b362-75351ff5d5d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8a/8af3fb742ff1ed334f8be319266dc2585d3c9675ec9fe959f77e165bfc7970c2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395547}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eaf619c5-ca27-565a-85dd-948767c05061" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8a/8a5f3a5965878cba364ee0e96af77bea41d53e6b2bd440340d72215e30db3a83.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395546} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc2ee015-3e7a-59a7-81ea-733222e4b837" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395542}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f792436d-ecaa-5cd1-8320-41fd8629919e" :question/math-academy-id "q-98709" :question/problem "A particular autonomous differential equation has the following properties:\n\n- $y=-4$ and  $y=-1$ are equilibrium points\n\n- $y=-1$ is a source\n\n- $y=-4$ is a sink\n\nWhich of the following could be the phase line of the differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nLet's check each condition:\n\n- We're given that $y=-4$ and $y=-1$ are equilibrium points. We represent this on the phase line by drawing two distinct points at these locations.\n\n- We're given that $y=-1$ is a source. We represent this on the phase line by drawing an upward-pointing arrow just above $y=-1$ and a downward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n- We're given that $y=-4$ is a sink. We represent this on the phase line by drawing a downward-pointing arrow just above $y=-4$ (already drawn by the above property) and an upward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\nTherefore, our phase line is as follows:\n\n![](images/8a/8a5f3a5965878cba364ee0e96af77bea41d53e6b2bd440340d72215e30db3a83.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395548 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b58b04cc-da5e-5c51-a583-0663d783c697" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Slope Fields Corresponding to Some Equilibrium Point Classifications"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "763068aa-2d32-5aa6-a823-b7ccf67073fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1a/1a9eb4ad4a219ae7b36e418fd51ab15de36940a67b5a8a4aeeef6538e18e551c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395550} {:answer/id #uuid "8c925e8a-1d13-5879-9258-7d0d5c968786" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b6/b6bbed5a54701e3e467e62fc1acfff78607c63b9d2fad07e092c826a3bca0c0e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395551} {:answer/id #uuid "b7936e16-6d5d-54da-833d-53150c5d80f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e4/e440928dfa83e3e653a2e508e9d01e53c73df84530402a393e82f56996eb5fde.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395552} {:answer/id #uuid "31d506e6-640a-5f73-80c7-b307d35e6ee1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/fa/fa06ae341ff2c761aa3e60de7270bd0ffad885b7e602c12c7af340acc0781818.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395553} {:answer/id #uuid "306b5ee5-c0bd-5f88-aaf7-65ca396796e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8f/8fd918e5eba15cec6472f84c64f6181920e8d02122919f844678f79aa23e27a0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395554}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7936e16-6d5d-54da-833d-53150c5d80f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e4/e440928dfa83e3e653a2e508e9d01e53c73df84530402a393e82f56996eb5fde.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395552} :answer-field/id #uuid "27088d4c-aa07-554c-843f-4a1dc5c26997" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395549}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "55d1795f-d6ac-5ff7-a9de-20bb6292950d" :question/math-academy-id "q-98781" :question/problem "A particular autonomous differential equation has the following properties:\n\n- $y=-3$ is a sink\n\n- $y=0$ is a node\n\nWhich of the following could be the slope field of the differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we draw the phase line with the given information. \n\nNote that there are $2$ equilibrium points, $y=-3$ and $y=0.$\n\n- We're given that $y=-3$ is a sink. We represent this on the phase line by drawing a downward-pointing arrow just above $y=-3$ and an upward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n- We're given that $y=0$ is a node. This means that the arrows on either side of $y=0$ point in the same direction. Since the arrow just below $y=0$ points downward, the arrow just above it points downward too.\n\n![](images/db/db7e9ea076c3a1fe2cf5341b841693e36d5b9a9030e624da88d2cfd3b0d3a1b0.png)\n\nOf the given options, the only slope field that correspond with this phase line is the following:\n\n![](images/e4/e440928dfa83e3e653a2e508e9d01e53c73df84530402a393e82f56996eb5fde.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The slope field is horizontal at $y=-3$ and $y=0.$\n\n- The slopes near $y=-3$ appear to converge toward this line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=0$ appear to diverge away from this line when we're looking below the line and appear to converge toward the line when we're looking above the line."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395555 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b58b04cc-da5e-5c51-a583-0663d783c697" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Slope Fields Corresponding to Some Equilibrium Point Classifications"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff8dd8d0-7d50-5734-b6ea-496d2a570e8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/57/5737491c0a472a12cb3fd9e0b09cb5ede88b193a3f084ca89671e4669b945d5b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395557} {:answer/id #uuid "d3cc9a9b-5ccf-500e-912e-e1f5b871939d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7d/7df58db225ecb32e6b51b2c16d78a557ca57e63f5d85551dfc1ddc85865e9ea4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395558} {:answer/id #uuid "a7af3a60-0900-581d-aa3e-62e9b0e7848f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4e/4e360e24357a714978fdfb64b239381b3d7c66d13a1307c265f067881aaebc54.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395559} {:answer/id #uuid "26e997a9-c909-5c38-8bf1-5444421cdf7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f1/f1302588da5388ebfa996005675625f98a9edee7c5ec0e99a2c897e70bd7e680.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395560} {:answer/id #uuid "3314cccf-cba5-50b7-a0ea-9fe1a2c4b4ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a7/a7961c00b93091e3bf775f6f61f503d9f3d16ad30174c4f3310654f5aa75721c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395561}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff8dd8d0-7d50-5734-b6ea-496d2a570e8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/57/5737491c0a472a12cb3fd9e0b09cb5ede88b193a3f084ca89671e4669b945d5b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395557} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f774429-ecb7-569b-bb60-6fa8f2847a27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395556}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a90c5eac-a818-5712-a0c3-9443f22bd769" :question/math-academy-id "q-98809" :question/problem "A particular autonomous differential equation has the following properties:\n\n- $y=-2$ is a node\n\n- $y=3$ is a sink\n\nWhich of the following could be the slope field of the differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we draw the phase line with the given information. \n\nNote that there are $2$ equilibrium points, $y=-2$ and $y=3.$\n\n- We're given that $y=3$ is a sink. We represent this on the phase line by drawing a downward-pointing arrow just above $y=3$ and an upward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n- We're given that $y=-2$ is a node. This means that the arrows on either side of $y=-2$ point in the same direction. Since the arrow just above $y=-2$ points upward, the arrow just below it points upward too.\n\n![](images/5e/5ea8af3b30df51189c3511dbe093f05b309dc50cbdea1e52d5ba3a42d88101c2.png)\n\nOf the given options, the only slope field that correspond with this phase line is the following:\n\n![](images/57/5737491c0a472a12cb3fd9e0b09cb5ede88b193a3f084ca89671e4669b945d5b.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The slope field is horizontal at $y=-2$ and $y=3.$\n\n- The slopes near $y=-2$ appear to diverge away from this line when we're looking above the line and appear to converge toward the line when we're looking below the line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=3$ appear to converge toward this line."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395562 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b58b04cc-da5e-5c51-a583-0663d783c697" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Slope Fields Corresponding to Some Equilibrium Point Classifications"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe98637d-b276-59da-b028-e7a79f6cf623" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b2/b291a1bb92db8f61a5c73274bdb65b5a3c3316c539a30f8539fef4fc763ab75c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395564} {:answer/id #uuid "14aab2ab-a733-5955-b8f4-c66687832d1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/06/06a3d9118f9e3c09f374760a6e8209b33c9458e4d4b476c56fae3c57f2ca3fbe.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395565} {:answer/id #uuid "495d9958-9a8c-5815-ae9d-4c89c5c9a473" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/fd/fd79ddf3d04b18f0b9740aa3a03edb3a335c351bc124095c8150aa1ad3f722e3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395566} {:answer/id #uuid "0b8826c3-8c91-5b54-8b61-9fd1b66e8e78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/01/01a3f9b8882698362729a25b874994e9e4879d797efde534b3cbbc0aec9d53c4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395567} {:answer/id #uuid "a74f555a-6ef2-5524-b8a5-3e85c80d995c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d9/d95d0ee9efde85e9c0751765a739fed7a1997df72c2caebd515c6622d22e3723.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395568}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14aab2ab-a733-5955-b8f4-c66687832d1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/06/06a3d9118f9e3c09f374760a6e8209b33c9458e4d4b476c56fae3c57f2ca3fbe.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395565} :answer-field/id #uuid "405fd9f6-6908-5082-b6cc-88b0df5a7ee6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395563}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f2360fff-fd15-5336-bdf4-c3f68971846c" :question/math-academy-id "q-98814" :question/problem "A particular autonomous differential equation has the following properties:\n\n- $y=-2$ is a sink\n\n- $y=-1$ is a source\n\n- $y=3$ is a node\n\nWhich of the following could be the slope field of the differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we draw the phase line with the given information. \n\nNote that there are $3$ equilibrium points, $y=-2,$ $y=-1,$ and $y=3.$\n\n- We're given that $y=-2$ is a sink. We represent this on the phase line by drawing a downward-pointing arrow just above $y=-2$ and an upward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n- We're given that $y=-1$ is a source. We represent this on the phase line by drawing an upward-pointing arrow just above $y=-1$ and a downward-pointing arrow just below it  (already drawn by the above property).\n\n- We're given that $y=3$ is a node. This means that the arrows on either side of $y=3$ point in the same direction. Since the arrow just below $y=3$  points upward, the arrow just above it points upward too.\n\n![](images/6a/6a05aa0192d5edcb3181e44a5e3c15f5514372c8c50f105e9c4bbae40d660118.png)\n\nOf the given options, the only slope field that correspond with this phase line is the following:\n\n![](images/06/06a3d9118f9e3c09f374760a6e8209b33c9458e4d4b476c56fae3c57f2ca3fbe.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The slope field is horizontal at $y=-2,$ $y=-1,$ and $y=3.$\n\n- The slopes near $y=-2$ appear to converge toward this line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=-1$ appear to diverge away from this line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=3$ appear to diverge away from this line when we're looking above the line and appear to converge toward the line when we're looking below the line."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395569 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b58b04cc-da5e-5c51-a583-0663d783c697" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Slope Fields Corresponding to Some Equilibrium Point Classifications"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d81a0bd-895d-587f-9430-18cda3288abd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/74/742d50bf56c55ad235ae96cdfc47db965f1e7b2197fee858d2fbac41299b34e2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395571} {:answer/id #uuid "889c0219-1cf9-5081-a7e9-c631d3bd55a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ed/ed931988a358942021c7030b0785c98df906cb40fbf676ac8c43a951d897c137.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395572} {:answer/id #uuid "d05f67da-7131-5757-8c95-a6b6ee1eefbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/63/6314a44b5c9b4e578e8af3c070b2bc04afe3a8db6efca14231c7f5b51582e1fc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395573} {:answer/id #uuid "f0fbbb3d-6a04-5db5-9384-e62ecf2481d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e5/e5e03b4c77e940170358f90fdbd8c7f7773095f323af533d94c0f64a8e5402c2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395574} {:answer/id #uuid "3a087cd4-416f-55bc-b424-3d0714460e53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9b/9b7f2410977d3373105b4c19630184a4a6356d9ae954d07c24567827b1541a18.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395575}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d81a0bd-895d-587f-9430-18cda3288abd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/74/742d50bf56c55ad235ae96cdfc47db965f1e7b2197fee858d2fbac41299b34e2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395571} :answer-field/id #uuid "75e090e2-ab8b-57ed-bf10-ff2bcbbd0cd2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395570}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9ffad951-1d28-599c-81ef-afd59eac5425" :question/math-academy-id "q-98816" :question/problem "A particular autonomous differential equation has the following properties:\n\n- $y=-4$ is a node\n\n- $y=0$ is a source\n\n- $y=1$ is a sink\n\nWhich of the following could be the slope field of the differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we draw the phase line with the given information. \n\nNote that there are $3$ equilibrium points, $y=-4,$ $y=0,$ and $y=1.$\n\n- We're given that $y=1$ is a sink. We represent this on the phase line by drawing a downward-pointing arrow just above $y=1$ and an upward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n- We're given that $y=0$ is a source. We represent this on the phase line by drawing an upward-pointing arrow just above $y=0$ (already drawn by the above property) and a downward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n- We're given that $y=-4$ is a node. This means that the arrows on either side of $y=-4$ point in the same direction. Since the arrow just above $y=-4$ points downward, the arrow just below it points downward too.\n\n![](images/9b/9b72426d7680ded1722e2f9270c96ac210fbe11cc1febd9390061177d239646c.png)\n\nOf the given options, the only slope field that correspond with this phase line is the following:\n\n![](images/74/742d50bf56c55ad235ae96cdfc47db965f1e7b2197fee858d2fbac41299b34e2.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The slope field is horizontal at $y=-4,$ $y=0,$ and $y=1.$\n\n- The slopes near $y=-4$ appear to diverge away from this line when we're looking below the line and appear to converge toward the line when we're looking above the line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=0$ appear to diverge away from this line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=1$ appear to converge toward this line."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167547 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd281121-21d3-5dc4-8537-af248d0fdf46" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Vector Field Corresponding to a Given Potential Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e1fa5bbb-0cad-5524-8a00-80b3cc811832" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xyz\\,i-{x}^{2}\\,j+4{z}^{2}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395577} {:answer/id #uuid "f7e38753-dc36-5e53-ae48-e8e105ddc9e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-xyz\\,i-{y}^{2}z\\,j+({y}^{2}x+2y)\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395578} {:answer/id #uuid "ce9c13bd-298a-540b-842f-319d1b0d07b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xyz\\,i+{x}^{2}z\\,j+({x}^{2}y+2z)\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395579} {:answer/id #uuid "00fabcdd-6a3b-5644-9c42-6c00944bb040" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}yz\\,i+{x}^{2}y\\,j-(2xy+2y)\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395580} {:answer/id #uuid "8d53317e-4f51-5b33-8e38-81c2f63e228f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xyz\\,i-{x}^{2}y\\,j+(x{z}^{2}+2y)\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395581}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce9c13bd-298a-540b-842f-319d1b0d07b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xyz\\,i+{x}^{2}z\\,j+({x}^{2}y+2z)\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395579} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c66c02b-c2b6-50bd-9cb2-f45f0ace8800" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395576}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9e4d7c82-2e83-562b-b6bc-3ec6c276e93c" :question/math-academy-id "q-98947" :question/problem "For which of the following vector fields is $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}yz+{z}^{2}$ a potential function?" :question/worked-solution "A vector field $F$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $F.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}yz+{z}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}yz+{z}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}yz+{z}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =2xyz\\,i+{x}^{2}z\\,j+({x}^{2}y+2z)\\,k \\\\  & =F\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\nF=2xyz\\,i+{x}^{2}z\\,j+({x}^{2}y+2z)\\,k.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125269 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "639785dd-cc89-52aa-84a2-84fc139c979d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Quadratic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32307806-5955-5592-95b0-6b2e3adfcb53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395583} {:answer/id #uuid "8add3fff-d190-501f-bb10-48b18eb21972" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395584} {:answer/id #uuid "55db6f99-f790-5443-95a3-e2e6e6ad6673" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395585} {:answer/id #uuid "d321d6af-443d-53c4-af2a-5534f866c431" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395586} {:answer/id #uuid "7335edf3-36b6-569c-82a9-31a630674e9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395587}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32307806-5955-5592-95b0-6b2e3adfcb53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395583} :answer-field/id #uuid "a37a1846-7305-5bee-939a-d49bb878e3e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395582}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4a77a271-f1dc-530a-b0b6-613b52cca69d" :question/math-academy-id "q-99040" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=4{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+5\\,$ on the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2y=0.$" :question/worked-solution "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2y=0.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨8x,4y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}8x=2\\lambda x \\\\ 4y=\\lambda (2y-2)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x(4-\\lambda )=0 \\\\ y(\\lambda -2)=\\lambda \\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2y=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-2y & =0\\,⇒\\,y=0,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,0),(0,2).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =4$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(4-2)=4\\,⇒\\,y=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2y=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical point $(0,2).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(0,2).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(0,2)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $5$ | $13$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at the point $(0,2)$ and its maximum value is $13.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069548 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea2f1348-55bf-5313-8d5e-a5a097231e97" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Stretched Reciprocal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e198b045-71f6-5a30-be49-c6b4f479eb70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/89/89233072deb8044ce467c1af083a3cafb680177efc602927fdd0192cf1c26fb4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395589} {:answer/id #uuid "d3456c9b-aaa6-50ec-89d5-87c6ee274774" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e2/e2a1274fc65341a659bb76923bb69677cc33bad1b78dfdf64ba97e0c8a00fb9c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395590} {:answer/id #uuid "0e245d7a-51db-5f34-9a2e-91237a92a01e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/5a/5a43656a35d47c7321e53d56238cd10f669bc807d57f72fcc002d79fb21aa111.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395591} {:answer/id #uuid "17de2bd5-c6bf-53fc-8728-38bc0d8f34a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f3/f3a1cac41b2f9d6773226d76722508e6d7f368a42a0f7e86a5d8407d6f42c149.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395592} {:answer/id #uuid "203a0de4-1599-525b-bb1a-6dd95576ec07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/79/79b496d28a0136f46e594a1b8dfddffcf4ed72557ded658259326e9ed09276ab.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395593}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e245d7a-51db-5f34-9a2e-91237a92a01e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/5a/5a43656a35d47c7321e53d56238cd10f669bc807d57f72fcc002d79fb21aa111.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395591} :answer-field/id #uuid "49245216-2c90-58ff-ac34-2c9d115029f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395588}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "99716dcd-78a8-57b0-9f22-e9b09ec2871a" :question/math-academy-id "q-99064" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=\\frac{3}{2x}?$" :question/worked-solution "To plot $y=\\frac{3}{2x},$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](images/a3/a32495fcca947d01cf8d3218fc5cfc0cce083a34e6fb6b629d92ba9e47039a6e.png)\n\n- Stretch the result by a scale factor of $\\frac{3}{2}$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=\\frac{3}{2x}.$\n\n![](images/5a/5a43656a35d47c7321e53d56238cd10f669bc807d57f72fcc002d79fb21aa111.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395594 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "52e11164-1929-594d-9f9e-460f6469421d" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Stretched Negative Reciprocal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e0e3c0b-cbaa-5dcd-b74a-2e4ad9731175" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9d/9da0db2e6c97e507e82b7ca801e46802fabf91ad31ff53ad3cc9ae580151370a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395596} {:answer/id #uuid "3588d9a3-473f-5e23-a0db-2cc2d3a6bc70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/20/20395852a0e436d54029140fe79c4feadd9de30b091f744256b2e2e628d27725.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395597} {:answer/id #uuid "aec56858-9712-5435-bf7b-e5dabfcc5418" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/34/344a3615421b88df05cb666ca90395991f3aa6af75c9095a60c73be89124b7e9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395598} {:answer/id #uuid "7652a3c5-36f9-5bbb-8c56-c79412ccfba8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/18/1813663317cf30286f540a84a5cba64248b2f268aa8c34cb3435f693b385c840.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395599} {:answer/id #uuid "ca73f64f-96cb-54ba-8c46-14f7b336eeed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ec/ec84a88fe3cb4b404c2332ae0cb05fe9fc5f34077505ba6b086420cae93b9c5b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395600}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aec56858-9712-5435-bf7b-e5dabfcc5418" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/34/344a3615421b88df05cb666ca90395991f3aa6af75c9095a60c73be89124b7e9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395598} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca574861-f9e5-5d61-af97-fd9563d42e54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395595}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a01e6206-bbdb-5e69-a533-c1319faf7313" :question/math-academy-id "q-99068" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=-\\frac{1}{4x}?$" :question/worked-solution "To plot $y=-\\frac{1}{4x},$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=-\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](images/9d/9da0db2e6c97e507e82b7ca801e46802fabf91ad31ff53ad3cc9ae580151370a.png)\n\n- Stretch the result by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{4}$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=-\\frac{1}{4x}.$\n\n![](images/34/344a3615421b88df05cb666ca90395991f3aa6af75c9095a60c73be89124b7e9.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395601 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5fa86dd3-3bb3-54d5-8940-5dc07ae4c99f" :knowledge-point/title "Exponential Growth Functions With Horizontal and Vertical Shifts"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7c4d435-ed23-5544-bf93-e1449ee399df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/03/039b46c639a4e4b6976c9d6d4ce8451a4206555d7d2960d3c822d669460dc8be.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395603} {:answer/id #uuid "7cbc6492-65db-5f5d-a611-c28f8b4d046c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b7/b75375bf734633beb27745f68c83db9b713d6be77a25ad778a55f989aa9e02fe.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395604} {:answer/id #uuid "d3d6b14e-40d1-5e9e-bdec-ed3828d1910f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/af/af07ec5cd9a09beebcdc833212f9b83d636ad79ac77e7a857068f458e778680d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395605} {:answer/id #uuid "25792ba5-676c-5168-88c6-f98127ebecb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/93/93806f8c1a1c51b2662abd62ee3bf6e82beb5009c7e53a6ef36dc154ca3dea82.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395606} {:answer/id #uuid "f3292f26-bf48-514d-93e0-3de4c68371b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/49/49d4c64247bcff847626b61eb8d35b1f0f8ae9e4ca31aafca78fa42198208b5b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395607}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25792ba5-676c-5168-88c6-f98127ebecb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/93/93806f8c1a1c51b2662abd62ee3bf6e82beb5009c7e53a6ef36dc154ca3dea82.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395606} :answer-field/id #uuid "d388ee46-9f1c-5f72-a233-48bf1d8d9a3a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395602}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "66873f54-8262-5038-8a99-c1ca86d4153d" :question/math-academy-id "q-99114" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of  $y={2}^{x-3}-2?$" :question/worked-solution "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- First, we plot the graph of $y={2}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/4f/4fa685525f4f08d801ff25bd0d7dc2ed94ca35b54424531a5a7d5568562234c4.png)\n\n- To plot $y={2}^{x-3},$ we shift the graph of $y={2}^{x}$ horizontally by $3$ units to the right. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(3,1),$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/3c/3c39774191845f83c6bfc1462771b9122ecde89ae0b33b550603d1d5007095db.png)\n\n- To plot ${2}^{x-3}-2,$ we shift the graph of $y={2}^{x-3}$ vertically by $2$ units down. The point $(3,1)$ moves to $(3,-1)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=-2.$\n\n![](images/ff/ff047be1b3289b16e4c64bafe256e42b1deebdd07c24694d0e5bcaba81710826.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395608 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5fa86dd3-3bb3-54d5-8940-5dc07ae4c99f" :knowledge-point/title "Exponential Growth Functions With Horizontal and Vertical Shifts"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae6f22bf-baa7-590b-a966-46ead3e6ca03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ae/ae3dc56985e60f0f7e87ccfa3d0e1740220f956367915d98dceb5ceae2f6f9c0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395610} {:answer/id #uuid "f876397d-fb5f-58f2-b586-e9951756cd76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4f/4f15654853012441572bbeaa571e06b4252f16567fdf2fd2de07e780d78e313f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395611} {:answer/id #uuid "efbfe314-5f43-58a3-a49a-084a4815843a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/6c/6c318f5bfa16ce2152d6cc8bc8321ccb0b5bd5d280a3d2ed8195e9a68ad8eaf8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395612} {:answer/id #uuid "f52be45f-71ce-5c0a-980c-09153bcd905f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ae/ae9e3767f5de41881a728c5b7e3caaf242852e7e92f57ecc949787d6176d10d8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395613} {:answer/id #uuid "31b0ddaa-fadc-5543-a76d-dd6399c7e5a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/21/21b54d5a2d87951c298840f99b69f872c64879ad38d7a1ca8736fbbb5aba9c3f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395614}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31b0ddaa-fadc-5543-a76d-dd6399c7e5a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/21/21b54d5a2d87951c298840f99b69f872c64879ad38d7a1ca8736fbbb5aba9c3f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395614} :answer-field/id #uuid "e292889b-68f9-5688-ac04-95c1887e9aaa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395609}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3e221163-d337-5112-9abe-1a301062750e" :question/math-academy-id "q-99119" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y={6}^{x-2}+3?$" :question/worked-solution "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- First, we plot the graph of $y={6}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/56/565fbb4450ba8feb0b1184cb18442ca00f9ce24d42d3d3243cfcc9aa82b62d23.png)\n\n- To plot $y={6}^{x-2},$ we shift the graph of $y={6}^{x}$ horizontally by $2$ units to the right. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(2,1),$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/06/0656969561de20752caa691f247f95f1daa743bb8bf167470e98c5d739b82b64.png)\n\n- To plot $y={6}^{x-2}+3,$ we shift the graph of $y={6}^{x-2}$ vertically by $3$ units up. The point $(2,1)$ moves to $(2,4)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=3.$\n\n![](images/cf/cf548121c884efcd7a36bc3282ae64d696f9de2da64b954bc2fa962376819477.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395615 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3081ffe7-c448-5e0d-8dd1-808d23199a7f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Definite Integral of a Vector-Valued Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0dfad44-fa97-5c53-b9c0-6604bf641ed3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨8,\\,4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395617} {:answer/id #uuid "0661ad18-e453-551e-b6a3-3b9a7fd4cc3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨12,\\,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395618} {:answer/id #uuid "9b12bbeb-f0bb-5166-9997-72fc2e3788db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨12,\\,4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395619} {:answer/id #uuid "d821aeda-21d3-5c5d-9e75-86510d821b5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4,\\,5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395620} {:answer/id #uuid "f706e041-e41e-54fa-8158-43e0674160c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨20,\\,8⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395621}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0661ad18-e453-551e-b6a3-3b9a7fd4cc3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨12,\\,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395618} :answer-field/id #uuid "41526ae9-61f1-5b5f-92ee-a7e06097aa76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395616}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b6a4c9ec-988e-50b9-a11d-a4ebe9c71ccb" :question/math-academy-id "q-99278" :question/problem "${\\int }_{1}^{4}⟨2t-1,\\,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}⟩\\text{d}t=$" :question/worked-solution "To integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each componentt separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}⟨2t-1,\\,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}⟩\\text{d}t & =⟨{\\int }_{1}^{4}(2t-1)\\,\\text{d}t,{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨({t}^{2}-t)\\,\\,{|}_{1}^{4},\\,\\sqrt{t}\\,\\,{|}_{1}^{4}\\,\\,⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\text{ }({4}^{2}-4)-({1}^{2}-1),\\,\\sqrt{4}-\\sqrt{1}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨12,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395622 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b049dea4-5045-57ec-ad6a-c49f30e5da1f" :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Matrix to Rotation-Scaling Form Given Multiple Parts of the Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5213c88c-facb-5418-8e63-52c97033d8ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395624} {:answer/id #uuid "c0e82d0f-0293-5d6f-aad4-c3f011e15dc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395625} {:answer/id #uuid "9b8aba97-b870-5004-9212-8297059c5900" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395626} {:answer/id #uuid "8997c136-51d1-5695-86d7-e0177dc3eb01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395627} {:answer/id #uuid "90edf21a-b251-56c0-8e28-5df70366c94c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395628}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b8aba97-b870-5004-9212-8297059c5900" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395626} :answer-field/id #uuid "33f2e820-62a2-58a7-938e-d07a21adae88" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395623}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3cb45241-4b10-5c8f-9bf0-52f914e1a848" :question/math-academy-id "q-99337" :question/problem "$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}7-3\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]=C.\n$$\n\nNext, we can find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =[\\begin{aligned}7-3\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(v)}+\\text{i}{{[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(v)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395629 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b049dea4-5045-57ec-ad6a-c49f30e5da1f" :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Matrix to Rotation-Scaling Form Given Multiple Parts of the Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20e41392-8432-5b22-aa41-0cdff945ff9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395631} {:answer/id #uuid "39141340-98a9-5f1f-9ceb-ac060eb31c83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395632} {:answer/id #uuid "ae413a9c-9e4a-59ae-929e-836cd2bae3ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395633} {:answer/id #uuid "4e2c5c24-6fc9-5369-b94a-28c4783d6010" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395634} {:answer/id #uuid "b902a495-b6c7-53b3-b3fd-d5da866abbdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395635}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e2c5c24-6fc9-5369-b94a-28c4783d6010" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395634} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef87be86-0ce8-57a7-8fde-951ee2f08f12" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395630}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7cb64545-86e3-59aa-b0fe-7ccf30353d96" :question/math-academy-id "q-99339" :question/problem "$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-2-3\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1+3\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]=C.\n$$\n\nNext, we can find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =[\\begin{aligned}-2-3\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(v)}+\\text{i}{{[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(v)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395636 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "53fca396-4ade-5180-9c9b-a73f24f5b5b8" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Vector-Valued Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f9abb18-03db-5798-a0a5-008c413ff8cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨t{e}^{t},\\,-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395638} {:answer/id #uuid "cdd0606e-6950-55d9-8f67-97852e598336" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨e,\\,2+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395639} {:answer/id #uuid "d4c48833-eee2-55d3-871c-819c80a63cec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨{e}^{t},\\,2-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395640} {:answer/id #uuid "bb4ef401-3b3e-5e40-9514-8b90f315a927" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨{e}^{t},\\,-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395641} {:answer/id #uuid "b2f6733e-81a3-5833-bd62-45bba158a64c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨{e}^{t},\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395642}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb4ef401-3b3e-5e40-9514-8b90f315a927" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨{e}^{t},\\,-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395641} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a1dc022-14a7-5d30-8e7e-1cdcc62ca9c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395637}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7f24cce4-3316-593f-909a-7953ae6cdae1" :question/math-academy-id "q-99350" :question/problem "Find $\\int ⟨{e}^{t},\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩\\,\\text{d}t.$" :question/worked-solution "To integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨{e}^{t},\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩\\text{d}t=⟨\\int {e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $x(t)={e}^{t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\int {e}^{t}\\text{d}t={e}^{t}+{c}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $y(t)=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,$ we calculate its integral using substitution. So, we let $u=2t.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}t=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nTherefore,  we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int y(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u)+{c}_{2} \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+{c}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨{e}^{t},\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩\\text{d}t & =⟨{e}^{t}+{c}_{1},\\,-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t},\\,-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩+⟨{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t},\\,-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395643 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3081ffe7-c448-5e0d-8dd1-808d23199a7f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Definite Integral of a Vector-Valued Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66b455d9-9dcf-5eb5-8c79-f1c4b2c17a8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,\\,\\sqrt{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395645} {:answer/id #uuid "e94dd2e1-84d5-5f65-9fcb-29972b9c2104" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-2\\sqrt{2},\\,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395646} {:answer/id #uuid "19fb725c-dfdb-554b-9ee0-5ea47a6fa6d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,\\,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395647} {:answer/id #uuid "4f175d91-95fe-5e94-86f8-2c07fa5e2233" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\sqrt{2},\\,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395648} {:answer/id #uuid "ac22ded7-29b9-55aa-a4fc-490f512ab8f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2\\sqrt{2},\\,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395649}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19fb725c-dfdb-554b-9ee0-5ea47a6fa6d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,\\,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395647} :answer-field/id #uuid "734ada2e-fef2-5740-8c5f-7c0d921da24b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395644}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ce4de9d0-a35b-5282-8a54-5ba3b82cc5ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-99366" :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}⟨\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{sec}^{2}⁡t⟩\\text{d}t=$" :question/worked-solution "To integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each componentt separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}⟨\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{sec}^{2}⁡t⟩\\text{d}t & =⟨{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{sec}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\,\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\text{ }\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi /4)-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0),\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\pi /4)-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\text{ }\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-0,\\,1-0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395650 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "53fca396-4ade-5180-9c9b-a73f24f5b5b8" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Vector-Valued Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18e40088-fc75-5f03-afdb-4419e14b0c86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨{3}^{t},\\,-\\operatorname{csc}⁡t⟩+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395652} {:answer/id #uuid "84bf4860-2cb7-5bfe-a96b-c459981516d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{{3}^{t}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3},\\,-\\operatorname{cot}⁡t⟩+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395653} {:answer/id #uuid "d0978271-3766-55c7-bd73-80b04d362f12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡3,\\,-\\operatorname{cot}⁡t⟩+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395654} {:answer/id #uuid "ed9d76f1-1a04-5098-a9d1-d93d96357185" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{{3}^{t}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2},\\,-\\operatorname{csc}⁡t⟩+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395655} {:answer/id #uuid "9e50a43d-3d69-5f77-b032-f2c078b14d12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{{3}^{t}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3},\\,-\\operatorname{csc}⁡t⟩+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395656}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84bf4860-2cb7-5bfe-a96b-c459981516d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{{3}^{t}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3},\\,-\\operatorname{cot}⁡t⟩+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395653} :answer-field/id #uuid "12919900-7f26-5b8f-ad34-67b95659b570" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395651}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "056c0a76-101b-521d-8188-2f2f8cee2913" :question/math-academy-id "q-99392" :question/problem "$\\int ⟨{3}^{t},\\,{csc}^{2}⁡t⟩\\text{d}t=$" :question/worked-solution "To integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨{3}^{t},\\,{csc}^{2}⁡t⟩\\text{d}t & =⟨\\int {3}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int {csc}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{{3}^{t}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+{c}_{1},\\,-\\operatorname{cot}⁡t+{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{{3}^{t}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3},\\,-\\operatorname{cot}⁡t⟩+⟨{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{{3}^{t}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3},\\,-\\operatorname{cot}⁡t⟩+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395657 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "88f3ba21-6407-503e-aec0-fa4462a69e73" :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Matrix to Rotation-Scaling Form Using the Complex Conjugate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4219457-908d-5c1d-94f7-a499e34c89f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395659} {:answer/id #uuid "33f641a6-0347-55ab-a00e-a1cf599920f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395660} {:answer/id #uuid "1df9a6d3-1b83-5494-b0c4-ae30de9f971c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395661} {:answer/id #uuid "4bddbcd7-63e5-581b-96a9-465fedef0af9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395662} {:answer/id #uuid "4b47ce83-6409-5bb4-ae4a-a2098cb1ee80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395663}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4219457-908d-5c1d-94f7-a499e34c89f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395659} :answer-field/id #uuid "a74f3e2c-c836-57fa-b4f6-b5ef43f0c419" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395658}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d1be07fe-c7c3-5b64-8061-f1dc9ad8baf8" :question/math-academy-id "q-99412" :question/problem "$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 1-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3-2\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\ne C,\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda })\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]=C.\n$$\n\nSo, our rotation-scaling matrix $C$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\overset{―}{\\lambda }=3+2\\text{i},$ \n the complex conjugate of $\\lambda .$ Hence, to construct the matrix $P,$ we use the eigenvector $\\overset{―}{v},$ the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nNext, we find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{v} & =[\\begin{aligned}2-\\text{i} \\\\ 1+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v})}+\\text{i}{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v}) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v}) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395664 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ef73a00-12f7-5818-85a9-d8b5e2582bfe" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the General Binormal Vector to a Curve Given the Unit Tangent and Principal Normal"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f9d3cdaf-2d78-5a76-bbc4-fcf0e22e276c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-1),0,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1)⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395666} {:answer/id #uuid "8f3bf5fb-639d-5222-907a-7159444e0f7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-1),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1)⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395667} {:answer/id #uuid "c095ef53-0f26-52d1-b64b-39cad54de695" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-1),-2,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1)⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395668} {:answer/id #uuid "c69c8048-96e8-540b-adc4-f674b1d6c9de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-1),-1,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1)⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395669} {:answer/id #uuid "865a20ff-d410-57de-be67-155ecdf21466" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-1),1,-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1)⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395670}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c69c8048-96e8-540b-adc4-f674b1d6c9de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-1),-1,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1)⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395669} :answer-field/id #uuid "e42d2c57-177c-58a1-b88b-e735ae9b0986" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395665}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4efd96a2-69d1-56ab-9a41-2279eca34149" :question/math-academy-id "q-99413" :question/problem "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at an arbitrary point, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-1),1,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1)⟩, \\\\ N(t) & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1),0,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-1)⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-1),1,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1)⟩\\times ⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1),0,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-1)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\,\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-1) & 1 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1) \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1) & 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-1)\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1) \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-1)\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-1) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1) \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-1)\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-1) & 1 \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1) & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-1)-0,-({sin}^{2}⁡(2t-1)+{cos}^{2}⁡(2t-1)),0+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-1),-1,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1)⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395671 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "88f3ba21-6407-503e-aec0-fa4462a69e73" :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Matrix to Rotation-Scaling Form Using the Complex Conjugate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e196512e-1c8e-5187-b969-8d1f8f8d5c47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395673} {:answer/id #uuid "3dcd7441-6b21-57de-a31c-17ca6a0f1cda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395674} {:answer/id #uuid "ee707b83-d226-5ed6-baf9-f30ac78abcc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395675} {:answer/id #uuid "6a5b4235-cc67-53b5-a8a9-129a5d3f6ab6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395676} {:answer/id #uuid "99e977e9-1f82-5a4e-93e5-ff723aa90c12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395677}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99e977e9-1f82-5a4e-93e5-ff723aa90c12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395677} :answer-field/id #uuid "619fbace-2c7b-50b1-a0f2-8ac4398e4712" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395672}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "57e563fd-7adc-5039-9d3f-4d9ce896a93d" :question/math-academy-id "q-99419" :question/problem "$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-3+\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 7 \\\\ -7 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-7\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\ne C,\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda })\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 7 \\\\ -7 & 0\\end{aligned}]=C.\n$$\n\nSo, our rotation-scaling matrix $C$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\overset{―}{\\lambda }=7\\text{i},$ \n the complex conjugate of $\\lambda .$ Hence, to construct the matrix $P,$ we use the eigenvector $\\overset{―}{v},$ the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nNext, we find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{v} & =[\\begin{aligned}-3-\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v})}+\\text{i}{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v}) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v}) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395678 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "91ea3125-c2b7-56d0-97d8-4e54b2c01524" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Specific Binormal Vector to a Curve Given the Unit Tangent and Principal Normal"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e39093c-b18d-598b-bb1d-e82594ff8e78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}⟨3,5,-\\sqrt{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395680} {:answer/id #uuid "db27c32c-4f09-5a20-9340-34bfb679cea3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}⟨-1,1,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395681} {:answer/id #uuid "c7ee025c-1e0d-5d85-8c01-8072d875ef6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}⟨2,2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395682} {:answer/id #uuid "4b9dc64b-7b3b-5b76-9ebb-e87ec5fb88e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}⟨\\sqrt{2},-5,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395683} {:answer/id #uuid "fac787ce-6139-599b-8b26-8c2d944beb1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5\\sqrt{3}}⟨3,5\\sqrt{2},4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395684}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e39093c-b18d-598b-bb1d-e82594ff8e78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}⟨3,5,-\\sqrt{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395680} :answer-field/id #uuid "c39daebc-26c3-5c90-9fb3-fb96ab3149a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395679}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d5570a23-d111-5812-8b43-05d4d1f22b84" :question/math-academy-id "q-99429" :question/problem "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $t=1,$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1) & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨-1,1,\\sqrt{2}⟩, \\\\ N(1) & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}⟨3,-1,2\\sqrt{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(1) & =T(1)\\times N(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨-1,1,\\sqrt{2}⟩\\times \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}⟨3,-1,2\\sqrt{2}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{12}\\,\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 1 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{12}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ -1 & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3 & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{12}⟨(2\\sqrt{2}-(-\\sqrt{2})),-(-2\\sqrt{2}-3\\sqrt{2}),(1-3)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{12}⟨2\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{2},-(-5\\sqrt{2}),-2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{12}⟨3\\sqrt{2},5\\sqrt{2},-2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{12}⟨3,5,-\\sqrt{2}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}⟨3,5,-\\sqrt{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395685 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "91ea3125-c2b7-56d0-97d8-4e54b2c01524" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Specific Binormal Vector to a Curve Given the Unit Tangent and Principal Normal"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cfa78f87-d5b9-578e-80cd-d4f3799e33a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}⟨4,-1,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395687} {:answer/id #uuid "3858875a-f356-55ea-93a5-f4c6f9a03185" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395688} {:answer/id #uuid "d90a6791-7e3c-5038-8b57-63839be796f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-1,1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395689} {:answer/id #uuid "e1d4a619-2198-5e09-80eb-d037451b6633" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,0,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395690} {:answer/id #uuid "2c180063-3d70-565d-b7c8-eea5d50da9f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}⟨2,-2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395691}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3858875a-f356-55ea-93a5-f4c6f9a03185" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395688} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc72f855-05c8-5ceb-9c2e-d29c0fca72ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395686}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e302d40d-778b-5d69-b79d-6c911b05e594" :question/math-academy-id "q-99430" :question/problem "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨0,-1,\\sqrt{3}⟩, \\\\ N(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨0,-\\sqrt{3},-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =T(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\times N(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨0,-1,\\sqrt{3}⟩\\times \\frac{1}{2}⟨0,-\\sqrt{3},-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & -1 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & -\\sqrt{3} & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ -\\sqrt{3} & -1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}⟨(1+3),-(0-0),(0-0)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}⟨4,0,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395692 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8dd18a5b-d7a1-5590-ae70-f47ff5b464b1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Antiderivative of a Vector-Valued Function That Satisfies a Given Initial Condition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91004853-1346-560b-a5d3-ff22bfcebfda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-1,\\,1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395694} {:answer/id #uuid "116a3ed5-0a5b-5b9f-9605-bf1f43cb3d01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|+1,\\,1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395695} {:answer/id #uuid "12041586-56b6-51e9-9f26-854cd04ef038" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1-t|,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)+2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395696} {:answer/id #uuid "007ec25e-10c4-57e6-8f6a-1b8e25331e7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-1,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)+2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395697} {:answer/id #uuid "9b87310d-4b2e-5dc6-a9de-c014aec90d95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}+1,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)+2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395698}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91004853-1346-560b-a5d3-ff22bfcebfda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-1,\\,1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395694} :answer-field/id #uuid "6907501d-a30c-54b1-9db2-2cf30d318d4d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395693}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e321419c-a366-5737-8cc6-0eb09e45ddb0" :question/math-academy-id "q-99546" :question/problem "If ${F}^{′}(t)=⟨\\frac{1}{t},\\,\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)⟩$ and $F(1)=⟨-1,\\,2⟩,$ then $F(t)=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that \n\n$$\nF(t)=\\int {F}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\int ⟨\\frac{1}{t},\\,\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)⟩\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTo integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨\\frac{1}{t},\\,\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)⟩\\,\\text{d}t & =⟨\\int \\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int \\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|+{c}_{1},\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)+{c}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe initial condition $F(1)=⟨-1,\\,2⟩$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(1)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1|+{c}_{1},\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )+{c}_{2}⟩ & =⟨-1,\\,2⟩ \\\\ ⟨{c}_{1},\\,1+{c}_{2}⟩ & =⟨-1,\\,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=1.$ Consequently\n\n$$\nF(t)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-1,\\,1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)⟩.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395699 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "93d895a2-73eb-5141-b56d-48090debf57b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the General Binormal Vector to a Curve Given the Unit Tangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "653e27c5-93b9-5b8b-a566-b9feceda02bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,0,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395701} {:answer/id #uuid "5794d3a1-89b4-5773-b8c5-d7d54c7628e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨0,\\,2,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395702} {:answer/id #uuid "4cce8c74-35bc-56c3-8c51-9d331c6a850c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-1,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395703} {:answer/id #uuid "567d70fa-56b5-55c2-b2e8-1f9b1ce38269" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395704} {:answer/id #uuid "7487841f-9614-5183-b897-83cb0df32a82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395705}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "567d70fa-56b5-55c2-b2e8-1f9b1ce38269" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395704} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff474b43-5d6a-5b7a-a58e-1b2570cb72d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395700}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c3acb097-24af-576e-b081-d26bf643475d" :question/math-academy-id "q-99574" :question/problem "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at an arbitrary point, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,1,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ denotes the unit tangent vector." :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t),\n$$\n\nwhere the principal normal vector $N(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nN(t)=\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+{(0)}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}})}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the binormal vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,1,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\,\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 1 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-0,-(-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}),-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2},\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
